# The Fire Code Already Wrote MWAA's Quiet-Airport Standard

*Draft v1 — Strategist. Prepared for airport executives, capital planners, and policy readers. Article, ~2,200 words, with a ~400-word executive summary at the top.*

---

## Executive summary

The Metropolitan Washington Airports Authority is about to procure roughly 5 million square feet of new and renovated terminal space at Dulles, plus a full replacement of the DCA Terminal 1 main concourse, on capital worth more than $20 billion.[^1] The question in front of the Board is not whether to write a "quiet airport" policy. It is whether MWAA will bind an intelligibility standard into the Design Manual before the Concourse C/D and DCA Terminal 1 consultant scopes harden — or bind it later, as change orders, on airline-recoverable rates that already forecast IAD signatory cost per enplanement rising from $12.77 today toward $20.71 by 2030.[^2]

The right instrument exists. It is called the MWAA Design Manual, which the Authority itself describes as "a mandatory guide with the force of law on the airport property."[^3] The right numbers exist. NFPA 72 requires voice-alarm intelligibility of average STI ≥ 0.50 per Acoustically Distinguishable Space and ≥ 0.45 at 90% of measurement locations, verified in occupied conditions per IEC 60268-16 (STIPA); ACRP Research Report 175 recommends the same floor with a signal-to-noise ratio ≥ 10 dB(A) for pier-style departure lounges.[^4] The right federal accessibility floor exists: 49 CFR 27.71 makes visual paging a grant-eligibility condition, and 14 CFR 382.53 obliges air carriers to give passengers with vision or hearing impairment the same information available to others.[^5]

What the ledger does not carry — and what this article refuses to sell — is any peer-reviewed measurement that a "silent airport" policy reduces missed boardings, missed connections, or IROPS recovery time. It does not exist. London City, Helsinki, Amsterdam Schiphol, Copenhagen, and Barcelona publish governance rules, not performance numbers.[^6] Even San Francisco's headline reduction — the U.S. reference — is a self-report without a published methodology; the traceable figures are 47% fewer paging events and 60% less announcement duration, achieved because SFO physically disabled airline access to boarding-area paging and centralized non-routine announcements at its own desk.[^7]

The recommendation is therefore not a "quiet-by-design standard" — that flag lands sideways at post-January-29 DCA — but a **Terminal Acoustic and Information Performance Standard**: mandatory intelligibility floor, mandatory redundant channels (audible, visual/digital, staffed) for every safety-critical and boarding-critical message, an explicit PA governance model with an IROPS carve-out, a tenant-systems chapter that reaches airline podium audio and concession televisions, and a two-airport variance clause. Order-of-magnitude cost: $12M–$40M one-time, or 0.06%–0.20% of the announced IAD program, at $0.03–$0.11 per enplanement.[^8]

