## Fort Lauderdale, January 6, 2017, minute 91

The shooter had been down for more than an hour. Five dead at initial contact. Six wounded. TSA officers were still walking the terminal when, at approximately minute 91 after the first shot, false reports of a second shooter rippled outward through Terminal 2 and beyond. Passengers who had received no authoritative message about anything for the previous hour ran. The stampede put more than fifty additional people in the hospital. The Police Executive Research Forum's after-action review, prepared with Broward County Aviation Department, described a cascading failure of mass notification and radio interoperability — not a failure of the shell of the building.[^9]

Fort Lauderdale-Hollywood International Airport does not appear in any silent-airport case study. It should be the first one. The injuries in hour two were caused by the absence of an intelligible, authoritative voice reaching a crowd already in motion. That is a terminal-communications failure, and every airport that permits its acoustic environment to be assembled by accretion — one PA head-end, one airline podium at a time, one gate handheld carried onto speakerphone, one concession television replacing the last one — is running the same experiment.

## The peer canon has no numbers; the fire code has all of them

The peer set MWAA is being asked to emulate — London City, Helsinki, Amsterdam Schiphol, Copenhagen, Barcelona El Prat — is a set of governance rules. London City removed final-call and paging in 2008 and points passengers to information screens.[^10] Helsinki did the same in 2015. Schiphol tells deaf and blind passengers to notify their airline "at least 48 hours before departure,"[^11] a pre-notification model that would fail a Part 27 challenge on the U.S. side of the Atlantic before lunch. None of these operators publish measured STI, LEQ, or intelligibility numbers. Their policies tell an airport what to write down. They do not tell an airport what to measure.

The measurements live in the fire code. NFPA 72 Chapter 24 sets the intelligibility floor for Emergency Communication Systems — STI ≥ 0.50 average per ADS, ≥ 0.45 at 90% of measurement locations — and IEC 60268-16:2020 defines the STIPA field protocol a commissioning agent executes with a talkbox and a calibrated meter.[^4] Virginia and the District adopt NFPA 72 by reference. A fire marshal already signs to it on every substantially renovated terminal MWAA opens. The quiet-airport movement and the fire code have been converging on the same terminal from opposite directions for a decade. Nobody has yet written it down.

## Alarm fatigue: the mechanism transfers, the numbers do not

Hospitals learned this earlier and paid worse tuition. Boston Medical went from roughly a million monitor alarms per week to about 400,000 by eliminating low-value categories. Johns Hopkins cut 63% of ICU alarms with one threshold change. The Joint Commission elevated alarm-fatigue reduction to a National Patient Safety Goal because 86 to 99.5 percent of physiologic monitor alarms are false and clinicians trained by the environment to ignore them do so at exactly the moment the environment produces a true one.[^12] The medical numbers do not transfer to a terminal — do not import them. The mechanism does. A holdroom that pages constantly is running the alarm-fatigue experiment on its passengers, and the message that finally matters arrives at ears that stopped listening two hours ago.

## Atlanta, December 17, 2017: the redundancy floor is federal

At 12:56 p.m. on a Sunday, an underground fire in a Georgia Power substation cut commercial power to Hartsfield-Jackson Atlanta International. Power stayed out for roughly eleven hours. PA and FIDS went with it. Announcements were made by megaphone, rebook lists were handed out on paper, and roughly 1,100 flights were cancelled.[^13] The lesson is not aspirational. Emergency communication head-ends, the airport public-address backbone, and mass-notification servers must run on independent UPS and generator power — specified separately from terminal life-safety load — or the standard collapses on the first bad day.

The redundancy floor is not a design preference either. 49 CFR 27.71 requires airport operators receiving federal financial assistance to display PA messages on video monitors so passengers with hearing impairments do not miss information others receive.[^5] 14 CFR 382.53 puts the same obligation on air carriers, which pulls airline gate-podium audio and airline-club audio into scope and refutes any tenant carve-out.[^5] ADAAG 4.28.3 is explicit that audible and visual alarms are both required and that a visual signal can only be observed within the space it occupies.[^14] "Reduce PA, add screens" is not a lawful design. "Govern PA, harden every channel" is the only design that is.

## The DCA overlay, honestly stated

On January 29, 2025, PSA/American 5342 and a U.S. Army UH-60L collided on final approach to DCA. Sixty-seven people were killed. The NTSB's final report, issued February 2026, assigned probable cause to the FAA — specifically, FAA placement of a helicopter route in close proximity to a runway approach, compounded by unheeded prior recommendations. The FAA restricted helicopter operations near DCA within days, and on March 14, 2025, removed the segment of Helicopter Route 4 between Hains Point and the Woodrow Wilson Bridge from published charts.[^15]

Terminal-side communications were not implicated in the NTSB probable cause. Say that on the page, before anything else about DCA. The reason this article is not framed as "quiet by design" is that framing would land, in front of an unforgiving audience, as an aesthetic decision at an airport still counting the funerals. Frame the same standard as *intelligibility by design*, present the emergency-communication and accessibility tier first, and its most publishable outcome becomes the ability to move a crowd with a voice message when it matters. That is the frame the Board can defend on a stage that now, per July 2026, includes the President and the U.S. Transportation Secretary.[^1]

## Concourse E, fall 2026: MWAA doesn't own the microphone

Concourse E opens in a few months. Fourteen new United gates, direct AeroTrain access, a 40,000-square-foot United Club — the largest United has ever built, delivered inside a Tier 2 program of roughly $898 million with about $235.8M in federal grant support.[^16] Picture the first winter irregular-operations evening in that building. A ground stop hits. The loudest instrument in the holdroom is not the MWAA public-address head-end. It is the gate agent's handheld radio on speakerphone, layered over the club's entry audio, a TSA callout, and a set of concession televisions that outlived CNN Airport Network but not its programming discipline. An experienced operating estimate — one this article flags as such, not as a metered figure — is that the MWAA head-end covers roughly 30 to 40 percent of the audible surface on a peak day in a large concourse.[^17]

A standard that governs only MWAA-owned zones moves the dB meter three or four decibels, not thirty. Which is why the Terminal Acoustic and Information Performance Standard must carry a **tenant-systems chapter**: fit-out acoustic criteria, re-verification after any envelope penetration, television and background-music governance, and a defined interface with the airline Use-and-Lease Agreement that took effect January 1, 2025.[^18] The SFO mechanism is transferable — audio-matrix modification plus a centralized announcement desk — but the SFO *headline* is not. Cite 47% fewer paging events and 60% less announcement duration; do not cite the self-reported 40% noise reduction or the 90 minutes per day.[^7] Dubai's 10-month, airline-by-airline negotiation, which capped announcements at two per flight across three terminals with no reported effect on on-time performance, is the ceiling of what governance alone can achieve.[^19]

The airline response is not theoretical. Denver's reported quiet-zone initiative was blocked by its dominant carrier — a single-source claim, but a directionally credible one.[^20] IAD's dominant carrier is United, which co-authored the Concourse E and the July 2026 transformation. DCA's is American, at more than half of DCA traffic in a slot-controlled airport where a missed departure means a lost slot, not a delayed passenger — one of ten beyond-perimeter exemptions the FAA Reauthorization Act of 2024 authorized and DOT awarded in December 2024.[^21] The standard that United negotiates into Concourse E's specification set becomes the baseline for C/D by force of precedent. The standard that MWAA writes for DCA needs an explicit safety-and-IROPS carve-out, or American will use the first irregular-operations event to invalidate it in front of the Board.

## Timing, arithmetic, and the one instrument that already exists

The Design Manual is the instrument. Seven volumes, 2020 edition, updated annually, with the Authority's own framing: "a mandatory guide with the force of law on airport property."[^3] The right amendment is a Terminal Acoustic and Information Performance Standard with a companion Specification and Estimating & Scheduling section. The right adoption window is the current Board officer term, effective for all scopes not yet advertised. Concourse C/D and DCA Terminal 1 are inside that window today.

The arithmetic is the least contested piece. Acoustic ceiling packages price at roughly $5–$15 per square foot in new construction and $18–$45 per square foot as retrofit. Applied to Concourse E's roughly 435,000 square feet, avoided rework is $1.3M–$17.4M, midpoint $6.8M — a single paragraph's worth of specification.[^8] A full MWAA-wide quiet-by-design stack — acoustic ceiling in the mandatory zones, PA head-end and zoning to the STI floor, FIDS redundancy across roughly 198 combined gates, a mobile-notification platform, and commissioning-agent scope — sizes to $12M–$40M one-time. That is 0.06 to 0.20 percent of the announced IAD program, translated to $0.03–$0.11 per enplanement, or 0.16 to 0.54 percent of MWAA's projected 2030 CPE.[^8] The commercial-construction late-specification premium is 2× to 4×, not the 1:10:100 heuristic sometimes invoked — use the range you can defend.

## The Contrarian objection, answered

The strongest version of the case against writing anything new is this: NFPA 72, ADA/ACAA, ACRP 175, and a competent commissioning specification already require what a MWAA standard would require. The Board has not asked for this. Standards ossify while personal-audio devices, spatial notification, and app-based passenger communication will mature inside the standard's first decade.

Each of those is true in its own sentence. None of them is true across the seam. NFPA 72 governs the fire-alarm envelope but not the routine paging matrix and not tenant audio; ACRP 175 recommends thresholds but binds no consultant scope; the ADA sets a redundancy floor but not a zoning grid; a commissioning specification tests one project. What the standard adds is *integration and enforcement* across the seam — commissioning acceptance in occupied conditions, re-verification after every tenant fit-out that penetrates the acoustic envelope, and a tenant-systems chapter that reaches airline podium audio and concession televisions. No single existing instrument delivers that. As for ossification: the standard is a Design Manual amendment on an annual update cycle, not a bond covenant. It is reversible in exactly the way a policy memo is not.

## The tiered decision

MWAA should authorize its Office of Engineering, in this Board officer term, to draft a Terminal Acoustic and Information Performance Standard as an amendment to the Design Manual with companion Specification and Estimating & Scheduling sections. Three tiers, one instrument:

**Mandatory across both airports.** Intelligibility floor at NFPA 72 / ACRP 175 thresholds, verified per IEC 60268-16 STIPA in occupied conditions at TCO and after any envelope-penetrating tenant fit-out. Redundant channel obligation — audible, visual/digital, staffed — for every safety-critical, boarding-critical, and IROPS message. Independent generator and UPS backing for the ECS head-end and PA backbone. Federal accessibility compliance (49 CFR 27.71, 14 CFR 382.53, ADAAG 4.28.3) written as design criteria, not aspiration.

**Recommended, terminal-context permitting.** Zoned PA with pre-authorized override, tenant audio governance including gate podium and concessions, hearing loops at gates and information counters (the Port Authority of NY & NJ's Design-Manual precedent),[^22] sensory rooms at each hub (Pittsburgh, Atlanta, and Indianapolis are the peers; IAD is behind),[^23] and centralized non-routine announcement workflow on the SFO mechanism.

**Context-dependent, airport-specific.** DCA carries a higher operational announcement floor because slot loss is unrecoverable and post-1/29 safety messaging load is elevated. IAD carries a higher accessibility and language-support floor because 10.53 million of its 29.01 million 2025 passengers were international, a share above 36 percent.[^24] The standard should say so on the page, and specify variance procedures rather than pretend a single prescriptive number governs both.

Concourse E is the first testbed. If the standard is not written into the C/D replacement RFP scope, it will be argued back in as a change order, in front of an airline that will not be surprised to see it, at a cost multiple this article will not need to repeat. That is a decision, not a study. The Board's window to make it is now.

